module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Descent.AssociatedModule.GlobalGenerators
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.GlobalPresentationOfPresentation
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ChartSliceQuasicoherentGenerators
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.QuasicoherentOverAlgebraChartGenerators
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.QuasicoherentOverAlgebraFreeKernel

/-!
# A module over a sheaf of algebras is quasi-coherent exactly when it is downstairs

Let `F` be an algebraic space over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf site `B`, let
`α : 𝒪_F ⟶ A` be a morphism from the structure sheaf of the small étale site of `F` to a sheaf of
rings `A` on that site, and suppose `A` is quasi-coherent as an `𝒪_F`-module, that is, suppose
`α` makes `A` a quasi-coherent `𝒪_F`-algebra.  This file proves that an `A`-module is
quasi-coherent as an `𝒪_F`-module if and only if it is quasi-coherent as an `A`-module.

That is the second assertion of Stacks 08AI read on the small étale site, for an arbitrary
quasi-coherent algebra rather than only for `A = f_*𝒪_Y`; the case of the tag is
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/DirectImageAlgebraQuasicoherenceCriterion.lean`, where the
quasi-coherence of `A` is supplied by the first assertion of Stacks 07U8.  The Zariski form of
the same statement, for a scheme, is
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.QcohLimits.isQuasicoherent_restrictScalars_iff`, and the proof below is
the transposition of that argument from the Zariski site of a scheme to the slices of the small
étale site of an algebraic space.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite_restrictScalars` — the
  hard direction: **an `A`-module which is quasi-coherent as an `𝒪_F`-module is quasi-coherent as
  an `A`-module.**
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_restrictScalars` — the easy direction: **an
  `A`-module which is quasi-coherent as an `A`-module is quasi-coherent as an `𝒪_F`-module.**
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_restrictScalars_iff` — the two together.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.hasGlobalPresentation_over_affine_chart` — the local statement behind
  the hard direction: an `A`-module whose restriction of scalars is quasi-coherent has a global
  presentation over `A|_V` on the slice at every affine étale chart `V`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_restrictScalars_free_over_chart` — a free
  `A|_V`-module on the slice at a chart is quasi-coherent as an `𝒪_F|_V`-module.

## Implementation notes

Both directions are local on the small étale site, by the clause of Stacks 03DN for
quasi-coherence, in the two forms
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isQuasicoherentOnSite_iff_exists_cover_hasGlobalPresentation` and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isQuasicoherentOnSite_iff_exists_cover_isQuasicoherentOnSite`, and
everything happens on the slice at one chart, where restriction of scalars and restriction to the
slice commute by `rfl` — they act on different parts of the data of a sheaf of modules — so the
restriction to the slice of the restriction of scalars of a module *is* the restriction of
scalars, along `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.algebraSliceHom`, of its restriction to the slice.

The hard direction produces a `SheafOfModules.Presentation` of `M|_V` over `A|_V` on the slice at
an affine chart `V` and feeds it to
`SheafOfModules.HasGlobalPresentation.of_presentation'`.  Its generators are
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.algebraChartGeneratingSections`, the sections of `M` over `V`.  Its
relations are the sections of the kernel of the morphism from the free module on those
generators: that kernel lives on the slice, its restriction of scalars is quasi-coherent because
the two modules it sits between are (the free one by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_restrictScalars_free_over_chart`, the other by
locality) and quasi-coherent modules on the slice at a chart are closed under kernels
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_of_isLimit_kernelFork_over_chart`), and it is
therefore generated by its sections over `V` by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.chartSliceGeneratingSections` — which is where the comparison of
Stacks 04M0 with the chart space is used.  Restriction of scalars then upgrades those sections to
generating sections over `A|_V`, by
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.QcohOverAlgebra.generatingSectionsOfRestrictScalars`.

The easy direction takes the cover on which `M` is globally presented over `A`, turns each global
presentation into a `SheafOfModules.Presentation` by
`SheafOfModules.HasGlobalPresentation.nonempty_presentation_of_isTerminal` at the final object
`Over.mk (𝟙 V)` of the slice, and pushes the presenting cokernel cofork through restriction of
scalars, which preserves colimits; the two free modules become quasi-coherent `𝒪_F|_V`-modules
and the cokernel clause on the slice
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_of_isColimit_cokernelCofork_over_chart`) applies.
Note that the cover here is the one `M`'s quasi-coherence provides and its members need not be
affine, which is why those two slice statements are proved for an arbitrary chart.

The per-chart steps are separate lemmas rather than inlined, because their chart has to be bound
at the type `(smallEtaleRingedSite B S hS F hF).C`: with the definitionally equal spelling
`SmallEtale B S hS F`, which is what the covering family of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.SmallEtale.coversTop_affineCharts` produces, instance search does not
find the category structure on the slice `Over V`.  This is the same effect recorded in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/QuasicoherentOverAlgebraChartGenerators.lean`.

